@meta
id: 26-03
title: Drill — range, bang, command
chapter: 26
type: drill
skills: filter-bang
par: 17
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: nothing that is one command
open: zones.txt

@brief
Eight times: sort lines 2 to 6 through the shell's `sort`, leave the
comment on line 1 where it is, save, quit.

Nothing to work out. What you are drilling is that `range` `!` `command`
comes out of your fingers as one movement instead of three decisions.

@keys
| `:2,6!sort` | lines 2 to 6, through `sort` |
| `u` | undo the whole filter in one step |

@hints
The range goes before the `!`, the command after it. No space is needed
anywhere, though one after the `!` is harmless.
--
`:2,6!sort` and Enter.

@solution
    :2,6!sort<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

By rep eight the interesting part should be the command, not the syntax
around it. That is the point: once the shape is automatic you stop
thinking "can vim do this" and start thinking "what would I type in a
terminal", which is a much better question, because the answer is almost
always yes.

@start zones.txt
# shipping zones, keep sorted
zone-east
zone-north
zone-west
zone-central
zone-south

@expected zones.txt
# shipping zones, keep sorted
zone-central
zone-east
zone-north
zone-south
zone-west
